The `!important` rule in CSS is used to give a style declaration the highest priority, overriding any other conflicting styles, regardless of specificity. When a property is marked with `!important`, it takes precedence over other rules, even inline styles or higher specificity selectors, unless they also have `!important`.

### Syntax:
```css
selector {
  property: value !important;
}
```

### Example:
```css
/* Normal CSS rule */
p {
  color: blue;
}

/* Higher specificity but no !important */
div p {
  color: red;
}

/* !important rule */
p {
  color: green !important;
}
```

In this case:
- The text in the `<p>` element will be **green** because the `!important` rule overrides all other rules.

---

### Key Points:
1. **Overrides Specificity**: Even if another rule has higher specificity, `!important` takes priority.
2. **Used Sparingly**: It should only be used as a last resort for overriding styles, as it can make debugging and maintaining CSS more challenging.
3. **Cascading Effect**: If multiple `!important` rules apply to the same element, the one with higher specificity will win.
4. **Inline Styles with `!important`**: If `!important` is added in an inline style, it will override all other rules:
   ```html
   <p style="color: black !important;">This is always black!</p>
   ```

---

### Best Practices:
- Avoid overusing `!important` as it can lead to messy and unmanageable CSS.
- Use it when dealing with third-party libraries or frameworks where you cannot modify the original styles directly.
- Prefer solving specificity issues with more precise selectors or reordering styles when possible.
